---
/** Where the 60 minutes a week comes from, and why the app no longer draws it as a bar to clear. Rendered by /science/movement/strength-training; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
---


<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Verve treats strength as co-equal with aerobic training, not as an
    add-on. In the UK Biobank analysis of over 70,000 adults, improving
    cardiorespiratory fitness and muscle strength <em>together</em> was
    associated with lower all-cause and cardiovascular mortality than
    either alone &mdash; the two protect through different mechanisms, and
    neither substitutes for the other.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Sitting today, as the tile counts it.</strong>
    An hour counts as sitting when fewer than 50 steps happened in it,
    from 07:00 to now; it is the same number the Movement balance row
    shows. Short, regular breaks help more than one long workout does.
    The phone only knows you were not walking &mdash; not whether you sat
    or stood &mdash; and a watch adds the standing minutes beside it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">The 60-minute figure.</strong> Public
    guidance asks for muscle-strengthening activity on two or more days a
    week; roughly an hour across those days is the volume the mortality
    studies were built on, and is the number Verve cites. It is a figure
    from population evidence, not a personal prescription. Someone starting
    out will do less and gain a great deal; someone training seriously
    will do far more, with the benefit curve flattening rather than
    stopping.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Why the app stopped drawing it.</strong>
    The Strength card used to show your minutes against 60 &mdash; a bar,
    a &ldquo;to go&rdquo;, a &ldquo;target met&rdquo;. For a beginner that
    reads as a bar they are under every week; for an athlete it is
    meaningless. The card now reports the minutes and the sessions, and
    the number lives here, where it can be explained. The same reasoning
    removed the 150&ndash;300 minute band from the Zone 2 card &mdash; see
    <a href={topicHref("zone-2")}>Zone 2 Training</a>.
  </p>
</div>
